        ecrm:P3_has_note    "The painting was probably originally a hanging scroll which was at some point cut down and framed. It is mounted on Chinese brocade dating to the early 17th century.\nThe painting depicts the Amitabha Buddha  seated in the lotus position on a lotus pedestal, his hands forming the virtaka mudra - on both hands the thumb touches the index finger to form a circle. He is surrounded by a halo and a golden nimbus. To his right in the foreground stands the bodhissatva Mahasthamaprapta holding a blue lotus and to his left, Avalokiteśvara carries a flask.  In the background stand two of the disciples of Sakyamuni: Kasyapa and Ananda."@en ;
